---
import SolutionPage from "../../layouts/SolutionPage.astro";

const jobs = [
  { name: "agent/fix-retry-logic · test", detail: "PR #611", logo: "machines", where: "Mac mini" },
  { name: "agent/add-rate-limits · test", detail: "PR #612", logo: "machines", where: "Office workstation" },
  { name: "main · build", detail: "push 4e8e89c", logo: "machines", where: "Zach's MacBook Pro" },
  {
    name: "agent/upgrade-deps · test",
    detail: "Every machine busy, sent to the next step",
    busy: true,
    logo: "blacksmith",
    where: "Blacksmith",
  },
];
---

<SolutionPage
  slug="ci-surges"
  title="Keep CI fast when coding agents push all day"
  description="Run GitHub Actions jobs on your own machines first and send the overflow to hosted runners, so CI keeps up when coding agents and teammates push at once."
  heading="Keep CI fast when everyone pushes at once."
  command="npx relayrunner connect"
  close={{
    heading: "Let your agents push all day.",
    body: "Connect a machine, then route your jobs. Hosted runners take the overflow.",
    steps: [
      { label: "Turn this computer into a runner", command: "npx relayrunner connect --share anyone" },
      { label: "In your repo, pick the jobs to route", command: "npx relayrunner init" },
    ],
  }}
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    Coding agents open pull requests around the clock. Jobs run on your own machines first and spill
    over to hosted runners when those are full, so nothing sits in a queue.
  </Fragment>

  <div class="sol-card" slot="visual">
    <div class="sol-card-head"><b>Last 10 minutes</b><span>14 jobs · 0 waiting</span></div>
    <ul class="sol-feed">
      {
        jobs.map((job) => (
          <li>
            <span>
              <b>{job.name}</b>
              <small class={job.busy ? "run" : undefined}>{job.detail}</small>
            </span>
            <span class="where">
              <img src={`/providers/${job.logo}.svg`} alt="" width="22" height="22" />
              {job.where}
            </span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
      <li>
        <span><b>agent/upgrade-deps · lint</b><small>Optimized check, no runner needed</small></span>
        <span class="where go-text">✓ Passed in 3s</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <section class="sol-band" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
      <h2 id="how-h">Use every machine first, and pay only for the overflow.</h2>
      <ol class="sol-steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Connect what you have</h3>
          <p>
            Run <code>npx relayrunner connect</code> on the Mac mini, the old laptop and the home
            server. More machines means more jobs at once.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Burst to hosted runners</h3>
          <p>
            When every machine is busy, the next job goes straight to GitHub-hosted or a faster
            provider instead of waiting behind the others.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Cap what overflow costs</h3>
          <p>
            Set a minutes limit on GitHub. Past it, jobs fail fast with the reason, and your agent can
            retry later.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap feature">
    <div class="feature-copy">
      <p class="kicker">Optimized checks</p>
      <h2>Don't spend a runner on a lint check.</h2>
      <p class="body">
        PR title checks and lint finish in seconds but still bill a whole minute and wait for a
        runner. Run them as optimized checks and they never need one.
      </p>
      <a class="sol-link" href="/docs/concepts/optimized-checks">How optimized checks work →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="sol-stats" aria-hidden="true">
      <div>
        <span class="label">On a runner</span>
        <strong>1 min</strong>
        <p>billed for a 6 second lint job, plus time in the queue</p>
      </div>
      <div class="on">
        <span class="label">Optimized check</span>
        <strong>0 min</strong>
        <p>starts right away, no runner needed</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap sol-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Common questions</h2>
    <div>
      <details open>
        <summary>Can bot and agent jobs use my machines?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes, when you share a machine with anyone in the workspace. A machine you keep for yourself
          only takes your own jobs.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which hosted providers can take the overflow?</summary>
        <p>
          GitHub-hosted, or a provider like Blacksmith, Depot or Namespace on your own account. See
          <a href="/docs/runners/providers">all providers</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do I see where time goes?</summary>
        <p>
          The Performance page shows how long each job takes on each runner and how long it waited for
          one. If waits grow, connect another machine or move a provider up.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do I need to edit my workflows to change the order?</summary>
        <p>
          No. You add relays once with <code>npx relayrunner init</code>, then change the order in the
          dashboard. See <a href="/solutions/routing">Routing</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</SolutionPage>
